From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] WM Jetpack: parallel view from the app's EasyGo rules

Many apps from China ship the split rules they use on Huawei tablets in
assets/easygo.json ("magicwindow" entity): which page opens on the side
of which (activityPairs, "*" for any page), a page shown on the side of
the main page by default (defaultDualActivities), pages that never take
part in a split (transActivities) or always fill the task (Activities
with defaultFullScreen), the split ratio (UX.windowsRatio) and whether
the divider can be dragged (UX.isDraggable). For apps with
OVERRIDE_ENABLE_PARALLEL_VIEW enabled, turn those rules into embedding
rules when the extensions load the app compat rules:

- custom mode (1): a SplitPairRule per pair, clearing the side when a
  new page opens from the main side and finishing the side page with
  its primary;
- shopping mode (0), where the pairs do not apply: one wildcard rule
  that sends every page to the side and pushes the side page to the main
  side. Launcher activities stay out of it; they are usually splash
  screens that start the main page and finish, and would otherwise
  leave the main page on the side;
- defaultDualActivities: a SplitPlaceholderRule, transActivities and
  full-screen pages: never split, the latter as always-expand rules;
- splits only in landscape parents at least 600dp wide, measured with
  the density of the window metrics rather than that of the app's
  resources, which some apps change; the divider is draggable (25% to
  75%, and to full screen when UX.supportDraggingToFullScreen names PAD
  or is "true") only when the rules say so;
- wildcard rules leave out pages with a floating theme, and implicit
  intents are resolved to find the page they start.

The rules apply even when the app sets
PROPERTY_ACTIVITY_EMBEDDING_ALLOW_SYSTEM_OVERRIDE to false, since they
are the app's own. Apps that use activity embedding themselves
(PROPERTY_ACTIVITY_EMBEDDING_SPLITS_ENABLED) are left alone, as for the
virtual gamepad. The SplitController constructor loads the compat rules
for these apps also when the virtual_gamepad_override flag is off.

Two app behaviours keep the controller from seeing activities, and are
worked around for apps with parallel view enabled only:

- some Application classes override registerActivityLifecycleCallbacks()
  and wrap each callback in a class that forwards only the original
  seven methods, so onActivityPreCreated() never arrives. If the
  Application overrides the method, the controller's callbacks are added
  to Application's own list, as the platform implementation does;
- some apps replace the Instrumentation, so activity starts go through
  an instance whose own monitor list lacks the controller's start
  monitor, or are passed on to the replaced instance. The monitor is
  put into the own list of the current Instrumentation, and of every
  Instrumentation it keeps in a field, when the controller starts and
  again before each activity is created if the Instrumentation changed.

Both use reflection on Application and Instrumentation fields; the
extensions library is part of the platform, so these are not restricted.

Full-screen playback: a page shown beside another one is in multi-window
mode, in which some apps refuse full-screen playback (Bilibili checks
Activity#isInMultiWindowMode() and only shows a toast). For apps with
parallel-view rules loaded, a page in a split of a task that is not in
multi-window mode itself is reported as not in multi-window mode, and
any other page as it really is (split screen, freeform,
picture-in-picture). ActivityThread sets this state only when the
activity is created and when it enters or leaves multi-window mode, so
it is checked after every layout of the page and again when the task
changes. The windowing mode itself stays multi-window, which window
management relies on for the bounds and visibility of the containers.

When the side container then requests a landscape orientation, as
video players do for full screen, the split is changed to
ExpandContainersSplitType without a divider: the side fills the task
with the main page kept underneath, and there is no handle over the
video. The previous split attributes are restored when the side no
longer requests landscape. The pages of the side container are judged as
window management judges the display orientation: from the top, the
first page with an explicit orientation decides, a page that requests
"behind", or a translucent or floating page without one, defers to the
page below, and an opaque page without one ends the search. So a page opened above a player does not restore the
split while the player shows through, and a player with a translucent
theme still counts. A split whose side page is a placeholder that is not
sticky is not expanded, since that dismisses the placeholder with the
pages above it. A page's requested orientation is the one it last passed
to setRequestedOrientation()
(Activity#mLastRequestedOrientation), else what the system holds for the
activity, queried once (declared in the manifest, or requested by an
earlier instance of a relaunched activity). The layout listener belongs
to the activity instance, since a relaunch can keep the window. The
SplitController passes itself to ParallelViewRules for this, and only
apps that got parallel-view rules are affected.

